# Multi-Model Synthesis: NHS Winter Pressure Improvement Priorities and Evidence-Based Interventions

## Executive Summary & Methodological Overview

This report provides a publication-grade synthesis of outputs derived from multiple Large Language Model (LLM) architectures and multi-stakeholder persona simulations—specifically integrating evaluations from **OpenAI GPT-5.6-Luna**, **Anthropic Claude-Sonnet-4.6**, and **Google Gemini-3.6-Flash** (incorporating perspective models across clinical, public, commissioning, and technology leadership domains). 

Across all evaluated models, NHS winter pressure is framed as a **whole-system capacity crisis** driven by interacting constraints: seasonal viral surges (influenza, COVID-19, RSV), primary care access bottlenecks, ambulance handover delays, emergency department (ED) exit block, inpatient bed occupancy exceeding safe thresholds (>95%), and delayed discharge linked to social care deficits. 

To transform divergent model outputs into a concrete, research-useful framework, this synthesis:
1. Normalizes overlapping terminology into standardized clinical and policy labels.
2. Disaggregates **problems** (e.g., acute bed occupancy) from **interventions** (e.g., Discharge-to-Assess pathways).
3. Evaluates evidence quality using a standardized four-tier hierarchy (*Strong*, *Moderate*, *Report/Policy-Based*, *Underdeveloped/Contested*).
4. Systematically incorporates **feasibility statements** across health system income tiers (High-Income [HIC], Upper-Middle-Income [UMIC], Lower-Middle-Income [LMIC], and Low-Income [LIC] settings).
5. Highlights frequently overlooked priorities (e.g., unpaid carer breakdown, cold-home energy prescribing, closed-loop referral ownership).
6. Identifies model-specific artifacts, overgeneralizations, and hallucinated citations.

