### Shortlist Priority 8: Respiratory Chronic Disease Management & Rehabilitation

#### 8.1 Pre-Winter COPD Action Plans, Inhaler Audits, & Post-Exacerbation Pulmonary Rehab
* **Concrete Problem:** Predictable winter COPD exacerbations driving emergency admissions due to poor inhaler technique, lack of self-management knowledge, and severely underutilized rehabilitation services.
* **Mechanism of Action:** Systematic annual reviews conducted before November: written self-management action plans provided with rescue medication (steroids/antibiotics); hands-on inhaler technique evaluation; systematic post-discharge referral to Pulmonary Rehabilitation (PR) within 4 weeks of exacerbation.
* **Evidence Classification:** **Strong** (Cochrane systematic reviews: McCarthy et al., 2015; Puhan et al., 2016; Lenferink et al., 2017; NICE NG115).
* **Implementation Level:** Clinical & Organizational (Primary Care Networks / Community Respiratory Teams).
* **Resource Requirement:** **Medium Resource** (£50k–£200k per PCN for dedicated respiratory nurse specialists and PR class expansion).
* **Global Setting Feasibility:**
  * *HIC:* **Highly Feasible** (Standard NICE quality standard).
  * *UMIC:* **Highly Feasible** (Low-cost, high-impact clinical intervention).
  * *LMIC/LIC:* **Highly Feasible** (Group exercise-based PR is exceptionally cost-effective in resource-limited settings).
* **Unintended Consequences & Safeguards:** Provision of rescue antibiotics without strict written action plans increases inappropriate antimicrobial use. Safeguard: Mandatory patient education on sputum purulence triggers.
* **Balancing Metrics:** Percentage of registered COPD patients completing annual pre-winter review; PR referral and completion rates; 90-day COPD emergency readmission rates.

